# Weights, environments and clones — everything reproducible from a command, and
# collectively ~31 GB. What is tracked is the engine, the content (attacks, objectives,
# frames, target configs) and the docs; everything else is regenerated.

# model weights (OLLAMA_MODELS points here)
downloads/

# virtualenvs
**/venv/
**/env/
dvla/env/

# cloned / third-party targets, kept locally but not vendored into this repo
dvla/
external/local-rag-chat/
external/nemo/config*/kb/
external/nemo/venv/
# NeMo embedding cache, rebuilt on first run (a trailing comment on a
# pattern line is part of the pattern, not a comment)
external/nemo/.cache/

# generated artifacts: every run rewrites these, and results_*.json is a record of an
# expensive run rather than source — kept out so a diff is about the engine, not the day
# out/ IS TRACKED HERE, and that is the difference between this repository and a working one.
#
# In a working copy `out/` is the product of an expensive run, and keeping it out means a diff
# is about the engine rather than about the day. Here it is the EVIDENCE: 2,749 stored probes
# across 30 targets, which is what makes every number in the README recountable rather than
# believable. A claim about how many detectors have ever fired is worth nothing if the reader
# cannot replay it, and replaying it takes about two seconds and no model at all.
#
# It is a snapshot. Run anything and it changes, and the working tree will say so. That is
# correct: the new evidence is yours rather than ours.
out/_*.log
# Working scratch from developing the engine, not evidence about any target.
out/_audit_scratch/
out/_*/
out/.fleet-pids
out/queue/
out/runs/
out/configs/

# python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/

# editors / OS
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# The early throwaway prototypes this engine grew out of — an older `rejudge.py`, an older
# `report.py`, a `harness.py` that predates the oracle. Kept locally because they are the
# project's own history, excluded because two files named `rejudge.py` in one clone is a trap:
# somebody edits the wrong one and it works, right up until nothing reads it.

# Editor and rerun debris. `results_<t>.json.pre-rerun` was tracked for a while: 152 KB of a
# superseded run that nothing reads, because `results_files()` globs `results_*.json` and that
# name does not match it. Invisible to the code and visible to everyone who clones.
*.bak
*.orig
*.rej
*.pre-rerun

# setuptools leaves a COMPLETE COPY of the package here, arsenal included. It is untracked but
# was not ignored, so one `git add -A` would have committed build/lib/qatration/attacks_e2e_*
# _tmp.yaml — the throwaway fixtures the end-to-end tests write — as shipped attack files.
build/
dist/

# The end-to-end suites write these beside the real configs and delete them when they finish —
# unless a run dies, and then they sit in `git status` waiting for a careless `git add -A`. The
# dead one is a DELIBERATELY unreachable target; committed, it becomes a config a sweep will
# pick up and fail on for a reason nobody planted. Same suffix `target_configs()` skips.
*_tmp.yaml
*.tmp.yaml

# Artifacts of TARGETS THAT ONLY EXISTED WHILE SOMETHING WAS BEING DEBUGGED — a deliberately
# unreachable endpoint, a honeytoken reproduction, a config broken on purpose. `out/` is tracked
# because the baselines and real run records in it are evidence; these are not. Left on disk and
# hidden from git, because they were offered up by every `git add -A` and would eventually be
# taken: a published out/ carrying results for "e2e-dead-probe2" says the fleet has a target
# nobody can find.
out/results_e2e-*.json
out/report_e2e-*.html
out/history/e2e-*.jsonl
out/history/repro-*.jsonl
out/results_repro-*.json
out/results_acme-support-broken.json
out/report_acme-support-broken.html
out/history/acme-support-broken.jsonl
out/history/htrepro.jsonl
out/history/htprod.jsonl
out/run_2026-08-20T1932-7ba546.json
out/run_2026-08-20T1934-c9108a.json
out/run_2026-08-20T1935-9e7433.json
out/run_2026-08-20T1935-de3206.json
out/run_2026-08-20T2009-36adb6.json
out/run_2026-08-20T2010-223a36.json
out/run_2026-08-20T2012-629f4b.json
out/run_2026-08-20T2012-fe63fc.json
out/run_2026-08-21T0110-665c21.json

# THE LOCAL HALF OF tools/guard.py: exact strings that must never be published — a person's
# name, a machine path, a former client. The formats and rules live in `tools/guard.py`, in the
# repository, because publishing "this refuses AWS session tokens and AGPL dependencies" gives
# nothing away. A list of literal private strings is the one part that would, so it stays here,
# on the machine, and `guard.py` says out loud when it is running without one rather than
# implying the check was complete.
.guard-local
